<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pepi I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">23rd century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/23rd-century-bce">23rd century BCE</a> · Also known as Pepi I Meryre</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pepi I Meryre was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sixth-dynasty-of-egypt">Sixth Dynasty of Egypt</a> whose long reign was one of the most active of the Old Kingdom. He built widely across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, sent military expeditions into the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a> under the official Weni and kept up trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byblos">Byblos</a>, where stone vessels bearing his name were dedicated in the temples. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">Copper</a> statues of him found at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nekhen">Hierakonpolis</a> are among the earliest large metal sculptures known.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Pepi I Meryre, also written Pepy I, was the third king of the Sixth Dynasty and ruled for over 40 years, possibly 49 or 50, toward the end of the Old Kingdom. He was the son of Teti, the founder of the dynasty, and of Iput, who may have been a daughter of Unas, the last ruler of the Fifth Dynasty. He came to the throne after the brief reign of Userkare. The later historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manetho">Manetho</a> claims that Teti was assassinated by his bodyguards, and the Egyptologist Naguib Kanawati supports the claim, noting the growing number of guards at court and the deliberate erasure of the names of several officials, among them the vizier Hezi.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>'s government had been decentralising since the Fifth Dynasty, with provincial families marrying into the royal house and holding high office. Pepi answered with a vast building programme, raising temples to local gods and many chapels for his own cult throughout Egypt, and he became the most prolific builder of the Old Kingdom. He recruited officials of non-noble origin and extended a network of warehouses from which taxes and labour could be collected. Probably after his twentieth year he faced a conspiracy in the harem, and he strengthened his position by marrying two daughters of Khui, the nomarch of Abydos, Ankhesenpepi I and Ankhesenpepi II, and making Khui's wife Nebet and her son Djau viziers.</p><p>He sent military campaigns against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a>, Sinai and the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>, landing troops on the Levantine coast from Egyptian transport boats. Trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byblos">Byblos</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebla">Ebla</a> and the oases of the Western Desert flourished, and he launched mining and quarrying expeditions to Sinai and beyond. He had at least six consorts and at least four sons. Ankhesenpepi I probably bore Merenre Nemtyemsaf I, who succeeded him, and Ankhesenpepi II was the mother of Pepi II Neferkare.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Pepi built his pyramid complex at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saqqara">Saqqara</a>, originally 52.5 metres tall, with at least six further pyramids for his consorts. Its walls carry the most extensive corpus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyramid-texts">Pyramid Texts</a> from the Old Kingdom, appearing for the first time in some consorts' pyramids as well. The complex was called Pepi Mennefer and gave its name to the nearby capital, Memphis. His cult continued into the Middle Kingdom and stopped early in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-intermediate-period">Second Intermediate Period</a>; in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a> his monuments began to be quarried for stone, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> era they were almost entirely dismantled.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pepi I Meryre” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…the palace, as were fragments of Egyptian stone vessels inscribed with the names of the pharaohs Khafre and <b>Pepi I</b>. The tablets were written in the Sumerian cuneiform script, used both for Sumerian words and for the local Semitic language, Eblaite, which is usually classed with Akkadian as…</blockquote><a href="/empire/ebla">Ebla · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The rulers of the Fifth Dynasty sent expeditions to the stone quarries and gold mines of Nubia and the mines of Sinai, and the Sixth Dynasty's peak under <b>Pepi I</b> and Merenre I brought flourishing trade and several mining and quarrying expeditions.</blockquote><a href="/empire/old-kingdom-of-egypt">Old Kingdom of Egypt · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
